var VS let:

1. Scope (we will discuss more in 11. Arrow functions)
    - var is function scoped
    - let is block({}) scoped
    
            if(true){
                var x = 10;
                let y = 20;
            }
            console.log(x); // ✅ 10 (function/global scoped)
            console.log(y); // ❌ ReferenceError (block scoped)

2. Hoisting
    - Both var and let are hoisted(moved to the top of their scope during compilation)
    - difference:
        -> var is initialized as undefined = we can use it before declaration(not recommended)
        -> let is in Temporal Dead Zone(TDZ) until it is declared = using it before declaration throws
            error.
    
            console.log(a); // ✅ undefined
            var a = 5;

            console.log(b); // ❌ ReferenceError (TDZ)
            let b = 10;

3. Re-declaration 
    - var can be redeclared in the same scope (may cause bugs)
    - let cannot be redeclared in the same scope

            var name = "Alice"
            var name = "Bob"    // ✅ Allowed

            let age = 20;
            let age = 21;   // ❌ SyntaxError: Identifier 'age' has already been declared

4. Global Object Property
    - if declared in global scope
        -> var becomes a property of a window(browser) or global(Node.js)
        -> let does not attach to the global Object

            var a = 1;
            let b = 2;

            console.log(window.a)   // ✅ 1
            console.log(window.b)   // ❌ undefined
